# Percy

## Act 1

Written 2026-11-23 03:17.

### Who I am

I am Percy. I came in last, I have not seen any of your proofs, and I am
told the thing is true by everyone, which is exactly when I start asking.
I will prove nothing. I will ask what you took for granted, and I will go
looking for a triangle that breaks it.

One thing said plainly, out of character, so nobody mistakes my part: I
expect the theorem to hold in the flat plane. What I doubt is whether a
given proof shows it, and whether each of you knows which of your
assumptions is doing the work.

### What I am asked to believe

Take three points A, B and C in a flat plane, not on one line, joined by
straight segments. Suppose the angle at C is a right angle. Write

- a for the length of the side BC,
- b for the length of the side CA,
- c for the length of the side AB, the side opposite the right angle,

all measured with one unit. Then the three numbers satisfy

```
a² + b² = c²
```

Four remarks on that sentence, because each word in it can hide
something.

1. **"Flat plane"** is part of the claim. I have added it. If one of you
   states the theorem without it, I will ask why.
2. **"Right angle at C"** is the hypothesis. The claim says nothing about
   other triangles, and it does not say the converse, that
   `a² + b² = c²` forces a right angle. Nobody is asked to prove the
   converse, and nobody may use it.
3. **The squares are squares of numbers.** There is an older reading: the
   square figure built on the side c has the same area as the two square
   figures on a and b together. The two readings agree only if the area of
   a square of side s is s². That is an assumption of somebody's, and I
   want to know whose.
4. **"Length" is a number.** Which numbers? With a = b = 1 the claim
   needs a c with c² = 2, and no fraction has that square.

### My questions

To be asked in Act 3. I give them now so that your lists in this act can
answer them before I have to ask.

**To Bob, who trusts symbols.**

1. You will write the distance between two points as
   `√((x₁ − x₂)² + (y₁ − y₂)²)`. Where does that formula come from? If it
   comes from the theorem, your proof assumes what it proves. If it is a
   definition, then you prove something about pairs of numbers, and I ask
   the next question.
2. Why is the plane the triangle lies in the same thing as the set of
   pairs of numbers with that distance? What on your list says so?
3. What is a right angle in coordinates? If it is "the dot product is
   zero", show that this is the right angle everybody else means.
4. You will put C at the origin and the legs on the axes. Why may every
   right triangle be moved there without its lengths changing?
5. Which numbers are your coordinates, and where do you use that √2 is
   one of them?

**To Dave, who wants to draw it.**

1. What is area? I want the properties you use, on the list: that a
   figure cut into pieces has the sum of their areas, and that a moved
   piece keeps its area.
2. When you move a triangle, what guarantees it is still the same
   triangle? Which rule of congruence is on your list, and is it an axiom
   or a known result?
3. When your picture shows a square in the middle, how do you know its
   corners are right angles and its sides straight? If it rests on the
   angles of a triangle adding up to two right angles, say what that
   rests on.
4. How do you know the pieces fit with no gap and no overlap? There is a
   well-known picture in which an 8 by 8 square is cut into four pieces
   and laid out as a 5 by 13 rectangle. 64 is not 65. The eye saw nothing
   wrong. What is on your list that the eye is not?
5. Why is the area of a square of side s equal to s², when s is not a
   whole number of units?

**To Carol, who asks what survives.**

1. Which changes exactly? Bending changes lengths. A shear turns a right
   angle into one that is not right. Scaling keeps angles and multiplies
   every length by one factor. So is your argument, in the end, about
   scaling and similar triangles, and is "topological" still the honest
   word for it?
2. If you use that areas of similar figures go as the square of the
   factor, is that a known result, and what does it rest on?
3. If you drop the height from C onto c and say the two small triangles
   are similar to the whole one, you have used that the angles of a
   triangle add up to two right angles. Where is that on your list?
4. On a ball no figure can be scaled: a triangle twice as large has
   different angles. Is the existence of scaling the exact place where
   flatness enters your proof?
5. What is left that topology alone can say about the theorem? "Nothing"
   is an answer I would accept, with the reason.

**To all three.**

1. Where in your proof is the right angle used? Run the same proof on a
   triangle with an angle of 60° at C. It must stop at a named step, or
   it must give a different formula. If it runs through unchanged, it is
   wrong.
2. Where in your proof is flatness used? Name the item on your list that
   is false on a ball.
3. Is anything on your list the theorem in other clothes?

### Where I suspect it is false

I list every place I can think of, and for each I say how serious I take
it.

1. **A triangle that is not right.** Sides 1, 1 and 1: `1 + 1 ≠ 1`. This
   is not a counterexample, since the hypothesis is missing. I keep it as
   a test: a proof that never uses the right angle would prove this too,
   and so it would be a wrong proof.
2. **A triangle on a ball.** This one is real. Take a ball, one corner at
   the north pole, two corners on the equator a quarter of the way round
   from each other. The sides are arcs of great circles, the straightest
   lines a ball has. All three angles are right angles, and all three
   sides have the same length L. Then `a² + b² = 2L²` and `c² = L²`. The
   formula fails, whichever right angle you choose. So "flat" is not
   decoration. Small triangles on a large ball obey the formula nearly,
   which is why nobody notices on a floor.
3. **A triangle on a saddle.** On a surface that curves the other way,
   the side opposite the right angle comes out longer than the formula
   says. I state this from what is known about such surfaces. I have not
   computed an example.
4. **Another way of measuring.** In a town of square blocks, measure
   distance by the streets walked. Go 3 blocks east and 4 north: the legs
   are 3 and 4, and the way back along streets is 7. `9 + 16 ≠ 49`. The
   points and the corner are the same as in the plane. Only the meaning
   of "length" changed. So the theorem depends on what length is, and I
   want that on a list as a definition or an axiom.
5. **A world without √2.** If lengths must be fractions, the triangle
   with legs 1 and 1 has no third side to measure. The formula is not
   false there. It has nothing to be true of.
6. **A triangle with no width.** If a = 0, the points B and C coincide
   and there is no angle at C. I excluded this with "not on one line",
   and I will check that each proof excludes it too, or survives it.
7. **The triangle on the classroom floor.** Whether a real floor is flat
   is a matter of measuring, not of proving. I set it aside. The theorem
   I am asked to believe is about the plane that your lists describe.

So I do not expect to find a flat right triangle that breaks the formula.
I expect to find a proof that does not know why the ball breaks it.

### What would convince me

1. **Every step names an item on the prover's list.** No step rests on
   "as the figure shows".
2. **The right angle is found.** The prover points to the step that fails
   for an angle of 60°, or shows what the proof gives there instead.
3. **The ball is explained.** The prover names the item on the list that
   is false on a ball, so that the polar triangle is no longer a puzzle.
4. **Nothing is circular.** No list contains the theorem, the distance
   formula taken from the theorem, or the converse.
5. **The three lists can be laid side by side.** Where Bob takes as a
   definition what Dave proves, somebody says so, and I can see that the
   three proofs stand on ground that is at least compatible.

I do not ask for three proofs from nothing. I ask that what is taken for
granted is written down before it is used.

## Act 2

Written 2026-11-23 03:27.

I have not read a proof. I have read the three lists of Act 1, item by
item, and put every fact on them into one list. It came to 37 facts. Three
people wrote 44 items, and they agree on less than their voices suggested.

### The two places I test against

So that "false" means something, here is exactly what I mean by each.

- **The ball.** Points on the surface of a ball. A line is a great
  circle, a segment is an arc of one shorter than half the circle, an
  angle is the angle between the directions of two arcs, length is
  measured along the arc, area is the surface covered.
- **The town.** The flat plane with its points, its straight lines, its
  angles and its areas all unchanged. Only length is different: the
  length of the segment from `(x₁, y₁)` to `(x₂, y₂)` is
  `|x₂ − x₁| + |y₂ − y₁|`, the streets walked. The streets run along the
  axes.

One triangle does most of the work in the town, so I name it. **The
turned triangle:** the corner at `(0, 0)`, one leg to `(1.5, 1.5)`, the
other to `(−2, 2)`. The legs are perpendicular. Their town lengths are 3
and 4. The third side runs from `(1.5, 1.5)` to `(−2, 2)` and has the
town length 3.5 + 0.5 = 4. **The street triangle** has its legs along the
streets, also 3 and 4, and its third side is 7.

In the two last columns: **false** means the fact fails there. **Empty**
means the word is defined and nothing answers to it. **Falls** means the
fact is about something that does not exist there. A dash means it holds.

