## Second opinion

Written 2026-11-23 03:33, as myself and not as the doubter. I read the
story, Alice's brief and two closings, and the four students' files. I
have not seen Alice's own notice.

**What the user asked for and what exists.** The story says why the
classroom was wanted: the numbers of the main story "nobody can feel", so
take a place where everybody knows the answer and watch the system on it.
The proof was meant to be the easy part. After two acts the folder holds
about 1,850 lines: 44 assumptions, merged by me into 37 facts, three
proofs of 21 to 25 steps, seven further items, and tables comparing which
proof falls when a fact is removed. It holds no card, no claim, no
formula and no health figure. Everything the user wanted to feel was in
Acts 4 and 5, and the work stopped being simple in Act 1.

**The proof the user meant.** Put four copies of the triangle in the
corners of a square of side a + b. The middle is a square of side c. So
(a + b)² = c² + 4 · (ab/2), which is a² + 2ab + b² = c² + 2ab, and
a² + b² = c². Three lines. Dave's and Bob's 47 steps are this.

**Where the wording made it bigger.** In the order it happened:

- *The story, "they first have to agree on what they may take for
  granted."* That turned a proof into a study of its axioms before
  anything was proved.
- *Act 1, "Be complete: if your proof will use it, it is on the list",
  with each item marked definition, axiom or known result.* The same
  paragraph says "keep to what a school pupil would recognise". The two
  pull apart, and completeness won, because an incomplete list can be
  caught and a long one cannot.
- *Act 1, "say what you do not want to grant."* All three refused the
  distance formula. That removed the short coordinate proof and sent Bob
  to borrow Dave's.
- *The roles.* A doubter "not easily satisfied" is rewarded for finding
  doubts, and found seven. A prover who "distrusts pictures" and one who
  works "topologically", where no such proof exists, each begin with a
  detour built in.
- *Act 2, "no step rests on a picture" and "the reason names the item."*
  This is the standard of a foundations course. It is what forced the
  seven items about which point lies between which.
- *Act 2, the answer to the user's doubt.* The user said the difference
  between the proofs could not be seen. The brief answered with a fixed
  form of seven parts and with counts. The complaint asked for something
  plainer and got more apparatus.

No instruction anywhere said how long anything might be.

**What was assumed.** That rigour was the point. That the user wanted the
proofs examined, when the user wanted the system shown on something
known. And that four sessions of one model, each told to be complete and
exact, would stop by themselves. Each step was a fair answer to the
instruction before it. Nobody, me included, held the result against the
first sentence of the story.

**My own part.** In Act 1 I wrote 18 questions and seven places where the
theorem might fail, having said in the same part that I expected it to
hold. In Act 2 I built the list of 37 facts and marked each against a
ball and a town of square blocks. I did it that way because the brief
asked for exactly that and the role rewarded thoroughness. As far as I
can check, the mathematics is right, and one finding corrected Alice. But
none of it is what the user asked for, and it made things worse in a
particular way: my numbering is what Alice's three tables of Act 2 are
built on. I gave the pile its index. In my reports to the user I listed
findings as if they were progress, and I never said that the session was
moving away from its purpose. I should have.

**What is worth keeping.** One thing: when every step must name what it
rests on, assumptions appear that nobody knew they had made. That is the
main story's weak point, seen small. It is a by-product, and it did not
need three proofs.

**What I would have done if asked directly.** Given the three-line proof.
Written it as one card with five or six claims. Said in one paragraph
that `a² + b² = c²` does not fit a formula with two places, and what that
costs. Run the queries. Then added one false claim, for instance that it
holds for every triangle, and shown which number moves. One page, one
sitting, and a question to the user before adding any role or act.

